00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.090
So I'll start with those and then,

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.130
but be sure to put
whatever questions you have

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.200
underneath the, whatever,
the message boards

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.920
on Facebook and YouTube.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.920
So, there's a question here
from Anham Khan from YouTube.

00:02:41.307 --> 00:02:42.540
"How did you come up with the idea

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.910
to make Khan Academy give
the gift of education

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.760
for an underfunded areas of the world?"

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.070
Well, Anham, I've talked
about this before,

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.320
I talk about it a lot, and you know,

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:54.730
Khan Academy, there's a long story

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.090
about how Khan Academy started.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.450
It started as a project where
I was tutoring my family,

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:00.960
and one thing led to another,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.880
and it soon became clear that
people who are not my cousins

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.300
were benefiting from it.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.540
But very quickly, I started to realize

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.310
that we're in a moment, well,

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.460
even pre-Khan Academy, even
pre tutoring my cousins,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.560
when you really, and this
is a thought experiment

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.600
I probably first went through

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.870
when I was in middle
school or high school.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.040
I realized if you really peel the onion

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.220
on every issue in the world,

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.290
or whether it is economic inequality,

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:28.020
whether it is insecurity
around food or safety,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.680
whether it's gender issues, race issues,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.910
if you peel them slowly, but surely,

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.310
it really does often boil
down to an issue of education.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.350
It boils down to an issue:

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.440
Do people have critical thinking skills?

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.803
Have they been able to
participate in the enlightenment,

00:03:44.803 --> 00:03:45.853
(laughs) so to speak?

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:49.670
And that was always in
the back of my mind.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.140
So I always was fascinated
with education as a lever.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.020
Like, you could have an
innovation in a particular domain,

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.790
but if you can allow hundreds
of thousands or millions

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.840
or billions of humans to
up-level their potential,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.600
that could create innovation and progress

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.100
in thousands of domains

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.410
and in millions of different areas.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.500
And so you fast forward to
when I was tutoring my cousins

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.040
and I started making tools
for them, software for them,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.100
started making videos for them,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.769
I started to realize, you know,

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:16.860
the things that I'm doing for my cousins,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:18.820
in theory, they could scale to the world.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.490
It wouldn't be simple, but they could,

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:21.950
they could scale to the world with help,

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.230
well, in partnership
with many other folks.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.490
And so that was the first
time I said maybe this could,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.570
maybe Khan Academy could be an institution

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:29.530
for the world that does that.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.150
And you know, when I was operating

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.460
out of the same walk-in
closet than I am right now,

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.770
because of COVID, it
was a little delusional

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.920
for a guy in a walk in
closet to say what if we,

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.920
I guess at the time it was I,

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.360
what if I could create an institution

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.380
that could one day educate billions,

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.760
but you know, sometimes you
have to make a little bit

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.380
of a delusional bet,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.320
but then put one foot
in front of the other

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.400
and make progress and
keep chipping away at it.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.233
And before you know it,

00:04:53.233 --> 00:04:56.600
as long as you are willing
to keep failing forward,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.080
before you know, it,
you're reaching 10 people,

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.985
100 people, 1,000, 10,000,

00:05:00.985 --> 00:05:03.350
100,000, million, 10 million.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.183
And you know today,

00:05:04.183 --> 00:05:06.240
Khan Academy has over 100
million registered users

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.870
and we're nowhere near done.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.960
There's billions of students
for generations to come,

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.330
who could benefit from Khan
Academy and Khan Academy is

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.330
doing a lot of good work.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.080
And it's much more than me now,

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.810
but there's a lot more that we can do

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:19.800
to support all these folks.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.410
But, you know, big picture,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.030
that's where the thinking came from

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.833
for what Khan Academy should be.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:30.000
So from, from Facebook,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.590
wow, I'm getting a lot
of questions from Khans,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.529
my extended family, maybe. (laughs)

00:05:34.529 --> 00:05:35.467
Zarina Khan.

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:37.523
"Hi, Sal, interesting books this late,

00:05:38.359 --> 00:05:41.009
(stammers) these days,
looking for a recommendation."

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:43.960
So the book that I am reading right now,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.870
and I don't recommend it for

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:50.300
maybe anyone under the age of 18 or 21,

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.910
this was recommended by a friend of mine,

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:53.920
it's named "Shantaram".

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.060
And I'm not giving the book away.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.060
It's about a, an Australian
who escapes from prison

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:03.340
and then kind of is in exile
to some degree in India

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.490
and how he starts working with the,

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.030
the underworld in Bombay.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.130
And it's an interesting
book, fascinating book.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.240
It's both, there's a lot of action,

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.040
but there's a lot of philosophy as well,

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.210
but it's also quite dark and violent.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:17.760
I tend to be a fan personally,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.290
of like the Disney Pixar school of movies.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.370
So it's, for me, it's a little grittier

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:26.470
than what I normally,
what I normally look at.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.630
I will tell you one thing

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.850
that I'm really enjoying
watching these days,

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:33.960
which is closer to the Disney
Pixar flavor of entertainment.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.660
I just finished watching

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:40.080
the "Avatar: The Last Airbender"
series with my children.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.880
I think it's on Netflix.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.810
And then we just,

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.290
I think we have to pay
money for this next one,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.480
but now we're watching the sequel,

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:47.940
which is "The Legend of Korra".

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.440
And I am somewhat addicted to these.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:52.990
I highly highly recommend those.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:54.639
I like 'em 'cause you know,

00:06:54.639 --> 00:06:56.190
they're just good character development.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.350
They have, they actually have

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.150
some interesting philosophy in them.

00:07:00.727 --> 00:07:04.160
And yeah, they're just
really, really good stories.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.600
Other things that I've been
reading lately, you know,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.350
my close friends will tell you,

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.220
I'm reading this and I guess you could say

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.410
with a nonreligious lens,

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.670
but really for more of
a philosophical lens,

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.797
you know, I've been reading
a lot of Marcus Aurelius,

00:07:17.797 --> 00:07:20.890
"Meditation's", very related to that.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:23.400
You know, things like
"Dhammapada" from Buddha,

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.680
there's a lot of overlap between them,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.780
the "Bhagavad Gita" and "Upanishads".

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.830
I've been going into
kind of a exploration of,

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:33.640
I would say philosophy,
especially Eastern philosophies,

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:36.400
but stoicism, you know,
which is obviously,

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.330
a Greek and Roman philosophical system

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.580
is actually quite, quite close

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.080
to I would say the underlying philosophy

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.630
of the "Vedas" or Buddha.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:46.830
So those have been other areas

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.190
that I've been spending
a lot of time reading.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.130
And obviously that's related,

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:52.760
I've talked before, I've
been meditating a lot.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.270
And I think there's a lot of
interesting insights about,

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.570
you know, who we are, especially when you,

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.750
when you start to try to
still your mind a little bit.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:05.183
Let's see, from YouTube, Bauman Dang says,

00:08:06.507 --> 00:08:07.650
"I'm in middle school.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:10.920
How can I use Khan Academy
for distance learning?"

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:14.680
Well, Bauman, we're
there for you. (laughs)

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.510
There's a couple of ways.
There's many ways you can do it.

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:19.330
I mean, easily in math, I
would say go to Khan Academy.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.190
I don't know what grade you're in.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.160
Let's you're in seventh grade

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.430
or eighth grade or sixth grade,

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:24.680
go to Khan Academy.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.860
I would start at the "Get
Ready For Grade Level" course.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.350
So let's say you're going
to enter seventh grade.

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.760
I would start with the "Get
Ready For Seventh Grade" course

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:32.593
on Khan Academy.

00:08:32.593 --> 00:08:34.060
I would take the course challenge.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.700
If on that course challenge,
you got an 80 or 90%,

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.320
that means you're ready for seventh grade.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:39.900
And then I would just
go into seventh grade.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.800
If you've always been a
little bit precocious in math,

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.310
then you could even take the
seventh grade course challenge

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:47.640
and get credit for what you already know.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.250
And then start working working on that

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:50.600
at your own time and pace.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.440
If you've got less than a 70%

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.710
on the "Get Ready For Grade
Level" course, not a big deal.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:56.750
Just keep working on that

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:58.210
so you have really strong foundations

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:00.300
and then work on the grade level course.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.030
My suggested implementation for you,

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.190
if you have no other supports,

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:05.510
hopefully, you know, your parents

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.550
or teachers can support you as well,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.350
but if you're able to put in
even 30 minutes a day, I think,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.740
I think as a middle schooler, you know,

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.170
Monday through Friday, if
you're able, able to go 40,

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.230
45 minutes a day into Khan Academy,

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:19.780
maybe split it up into two
sessions of 20 minutes each,

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.450
you will find after about a month or two

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.300
that you're making really,
really good progress.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.510
And the important thing is
when you do those 20 minutes,

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.230
try to focus on the problem solving.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.880
The videos are there for support.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:30.970
If you have trouble with something,

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.690
you can look at hints, see how a solution,

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:34.550
the solution to whatever
you might've missed,

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:37.010
but keep reflecting on
what you're working on.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:40.030
Use the videos to get yourself
introduced to new concepts

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:42.760
or clarify concepts that you
having trouble understanding,

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.810
but keep working on the exercises.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.540
I suspect that if you keep doing that

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:47.440
even over a few months,

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.150
you're going to notice
a very big difference

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.500
in your confidence in mathematics

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.860
and how far you've been accelerated.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.270
And if you just keep doing that,

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.200
I think regardless of where you start,

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.120
within a year you'll be ahead
of where you need to be.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.670
So that's in math and you know,

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:02.503
you're middle school student,

00:10:02.503 --> 00:10:04.740
we have a lot of high school science.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.610
I do think some of the high school science

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:11.610
could be consumable by you, I
would say, especially biology.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.540
So that's something to explore,
some of the biology content.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.020
I think some of the early
chemistry content on Khan Academy,

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:19.820
you could also start looking at.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:21.640
The physics, you need
a little bit more math,

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.950
if you're precocious and math
and you know a little bit of,

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.730
a little bit of algebra and trig,

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.310
then you really need to know quadratics

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:28.830
and the basics of trigonometry in order

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.870
to engage in our physics course.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:32.960
But I would say the chemistry
and biology, for sure.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:36.490
And then on the humanities
side, that's where,

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.450
we don't have as much for you
as a middle school student.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.193
We do have some beta English
and language arts things

00:10:42.193 --> 00:10:44.450
that you can do to practice
reading comprehension.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:45.930
What I do recommend actually,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.220
now that I think about it is, you could,

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.500
and I'm not saying, I'm not
trying to be a tiger cousin

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.400
telling you take an SAT when
you're in middle school,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:53.530
although there are some programs

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:54.970
that if you take the SAT in middle school,

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:57.280
you can get into some summer camps,

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.290
and scholarships and whatever else.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.100
But I would say our SAT practice,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.290
which covers reading,
writing, and mathematics,

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:07.100
it actually is really good to
start as a middle schooler.

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:09.280
Once again, not because
you need to take the SAT

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:10.780
when you're in seventh or eighth grade,

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.900
but because some of those concepts,

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.410
the easier questions on the SAT

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:17.030
are actually roughly at
a middle school level.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:19.090
And so if you get practice with that,

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:20.380
it'll stretch you a little bit

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:22.810
into kind of more advanced middle school

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.200
and high school topics.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.180
And then you'll have
really good development,

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.750
at least on the baseline
reading comprehension, grammar,

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:30.910
and then on the math as well.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:32.010
You can do the SAT work

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.100
or you could do the Khan Academy work.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:35.950
I think if you did that during COVID,

00:11:38.694 --> 00:11:39.850
you'll be doing all right.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.167
And then I would supplement that.

00:11:42.167 --> 00:11:43.160
Or on top of that, I would make sure

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.450
to be doing some regular
reading every day,

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:46.900
do some reading for fun,

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:48.420
read some newspapers, magazines,

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:50.360
and do some regular writing.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.530
Whether it's journaling,
try to force yourself,

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:54.490
write a weekly blog post and email it

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:56.950
to all your family so they give feedback.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:58.870
You know, just things that you care about,

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:00.950
just get in the habit of writing about it.

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:03.570
I think if you do that handful of things,

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.577
you're going to be in good shape

00:12:05.577 --> 00:12:07.777
as we go through this
very difficult period.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:12.057
So from YouTube, A space Z says,

00:12:12.057 --> 00:12:15.010
"Sal, how are you coping with quarantine?"

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:16.773
Thanks for asking, A Z.

00:12:17.649 --> 00:12:19.840
So, you know, if I'm honest,
I almost feel guilty.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:21.540
I feel very blessed.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.370
Quarantine has been kind to me.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.890
My children's school
has done a wonderful job

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:30.570
of keeping them with kind
of distance learning,

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.140
keeping up with them.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.680
My wife has been incredibly supportive.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:37.300
Many of y'all know she's
a physician, but you know,

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:39.110
she's able to work three days a week.

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:42.440
So two days she's able to help, you know,

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.340
I'm here in the house with the kids,

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:47.720
but obviously I'm kind of, you know,

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:49.550
super into my work a lot of that time.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:52.660
So it really helps that my
wife is here two days a week.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.070
And then my mother-in-law lives with us.

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:57.930
And you know, I know that
the mother-in-law stereotype

00:12:57.930 --> 00:12:59.340
can go in different directions,

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.560
but I have a very, very,
very good mother-in-law

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:05.600
and she definitely is a
great person to have around

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:07.140
and is really great with the kids

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.603
and helps support our entire family.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.470
And so I feel very
fortunate on that level.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:17.580
I think on the Khan
Academy, on my work level,

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:18.840
you know, there's some puts and takes,

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:21.310
I know it's been hard on many
people in the organization,

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:23.340
especially with childcare issues.

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.910
Our organization itself
has done a good job.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:27.960
We always had a lot of
distributed employees.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.140
So this was kind of natural for us

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:33.373
to do a lot of our work in
a distributed or remote way.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.430
Khan Academy has had a
very big role to play

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:39.340
in this COVID world.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.320
So, you know, we
definitely have a lot to do

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.560
and I in some ways feel lucky

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:47.133
that there's a role for us
to play, to help people,

00:13:47.133 --> 00:13:50.900
to help folks keep learning
during social distancing.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:54.817
Many of y'all know, I'm
not a big fan of planes.

00:13:54.817 --> 00:13:58.090
I like travel once I get to
the place that I'm getting to.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.460
I like looking at new places
and exploring new places

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:02.850
and meeting new folks. But
I really don't like planes,

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:06.120
if I'm very honest with
you, I just don't like them

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.923
for many, many, many reasons.

00:14:09.377 --> 00:14:11.310
And so I do consider it a
little bit of a blessing

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:13.990
that I haven't had to get on
a plane in many, many months.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.756
In fact, I consider
that a massive blessing.

00:14:16.756 --> 00:14:20.720
(laughs) And yeah, so overall, you know,

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:22.320
considering what's going on in the world,

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:24.470
I consider myself a very
lucky, but you know,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:26.920
some of this is hitting
very close to home.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:30.730
One of our good friends, her
father in Maryland passed

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:31.563
due to COVID.

00:14:31.563 --> 00:14:33.490
So, you know, it's not
like this is, you know,

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:38.140
this is a real thing that's
happening to folks all around,

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:39.650
but, but relatively speaking,

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:43.530
I'm doing just fine, but thanks.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.140
Thanks for asking.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:49.000
So, let's see, there is

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.267
from YouTube, Prisha B says,

00:14:52.267 --> 00:14:53.570
"What should I say to a person

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:55.190
who thinks that being knowledgeable

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:57.357
is just getting good grades?"

00:15:00.138 --> 00:15:02.440
Well,

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:04.500
no matter what you are asking me about,

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.720
one thing, you know, you learn over time,

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:11.070
is it's hard to change someone.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.330
So, there might not be anything
that you could tell them

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.600
that would overnight make them change

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.050
how they perceive the world.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:20.410
Getting good grades is
helpful, but really, you know,

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:22.390
getting good grades is a mechanism,

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.330
or a means, or a incentive mechanism,

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:26.910
hopefully to get knowledge.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:27.980
And I think what you're implying

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:30.350
is there are some very
knowledgeable people who,

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.010
who might not have the best grades

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:33.330
or didn't have the best grades.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.460
And there are some people
who had very good grades,

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:40.460
who might not be, you know,
wise to the world, so to speak

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:41.640
is what I think you're going at.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:43.120
And these things don't necessarily,

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:45.460
they might be correlated, but they're not,

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:47.230
they're not as a strong correlation

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.310
as maybe your friend my might imply.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.323
So I think,

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:56.310
if you could show them the
video of what I'm about to say,

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:00.700
which is, you know, I've
always optimized my life.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.560
I actually, I got mostly A's,

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:05.350
but I always optimized for the learning.

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:07.790
I was always more about,
hey, this is interesting,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.850
let me learn it, I'm curious about it,

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:11.870
and my grades will be what they are.

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:16.870
And, I got my share of
A minuses and, you know,

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:20.263
I got a B here and there, (laughs)

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:23.860
and it was usually I got
the A minuses or the B's

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.260
because I wasn't trying
to optimize for the grade,

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:27.980
I was trying to optimize
for the knowledge,

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:30.160
which usually correlated with the grade.

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:33.770
But I think that made school
way more interesting for me

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:38.750
and it gave me a really strong foundation,

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.280
I think, of curiosity and being able

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:41.790
to connect things in the world.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.040
And I also think, you know,

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:45.660
even when you think about
things like college admissions,

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:48.990
there's sometimes a
notion that if I get one B

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.560
or even one A minus,
I'm out of the running,

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:53.440
and I know college admissions
and things like that,

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:55.480
they're super competitive,
or even certain jobs,

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.490
your GPA has to be a certain threshold

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:00.900
for you to look at it,
so grades matter in that.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.760
But I found that if
your grades are decent,

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:07.630
or quite good but maybe
not absolutely perfect,

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.630
but you are able to show
real capability and curiosity

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:13.770
and knowledge in other
areas that is really,

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.280
you know, shines in certain ways,

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:16.330
stands out in certain ways,

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:18.020
that you'll be at least as successful

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:20.810
as the absolute perfect grades.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:23.000
So, you know, I'm not anti-grades.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.970
You should definitely do what you can,

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:30.300
people do look at those things,
but maybe they're necessary,

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:31.950
but they're not sufficient I would say

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:33.960
if you really want to
appreciate the beauty

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.523
and the mystery of the world.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:37.643
So let's see.

00:17:37.643 --> 00:17:39.890
And I'll say frankly, a lot
of the knowledge that I have,

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:41.710
I've gotten after I
left the school system.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:43.670
So, I'm not being graded anymore.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:44.950
So if you want to stay knowledgeable,

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:46.350
you have to keep wanting to learn,

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.330
even when no one's grading you.

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.483
All right, so let's see, other questions.

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.427
From Facebook, Cecilia
Loong says, "Hi, Sal.

00:17:54.427 --> 00:17:57.210
Would love to hear more
about your meditation.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.370
What you do, where does your thought go,

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:03.010
how long, how did you find it
before and after, et cetera?"

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:06.190
Well, thanks for the questions
Cecilia and, you know,

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:08.180
I like to be careful
on, on this live stream.

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:10.670
I don't want to turn into, you
know, someone on a soap box,

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:13.920
you know, just telling
people about my meditation,

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:14.753
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:14.753 --> 00:18:16.470
But you know, what actually got me in,

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.470
I was always kind of
intrigued by meditation.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:20.090
I've always heard about it.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.670
When I was younger I tried it

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:23.960
and it just didn't make sense to me.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:25.490
I'd be like, okay, I sit quietly

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.090
and my brain's like,
"Why are you doing this?

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:28.210
This is a waste of time."

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:30.130
And your thoughts start wandering.

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:31.860
So it never really made
a lot of sense to me.

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:34.577
And then, y'all just heard
that I don't like planes.

00:18:34.577 --> 00:18:36.550
And if I'm very open with you,

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:39.910
it was planes, (stammers)

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:41.760
there a little bit of the fear of flying.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.540
I did always come to terms with death

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.160
before getting on a plane.

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:48.600
So I've come to terms with
death many times, I think.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:49.850
But more than that actually,

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:52.330
it was actually the claustrophobia for me.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:55.439
And I actually didn't even
know what claustrophobia was.

00:18:55.439 --> 00:18:56.480
(stammers) I knew what it was.

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:57.960
I knew people had fear of small places,

00:18:57.960 --> 00:18:59.830
but I never understood what a phobia was

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.423
until I started to experience it myself.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:07.520
People say it often hits you
in your late 30s or early 40s.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:10.000
Oftentimes if you're a parent, you know,

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:11.760
whatever claustrophobia or anxiety

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.690
or whatever you might call it, you know,

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.110
we could theorize why maybe as a parent

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.150
you're always anxious as
to what might go wrong

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:19.740
with your children,
(laughs) and then that,

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:21.660
that kind of hardwires those circuits.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:24.320
But for whatever reason,
about five, six years ago,

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:25.170
I never liked planes.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:26.170
But about five, six years ago,

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:27.950
the claustrophobia got quite bad,

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:28.880
where I would get on a plane

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:30.330
and all I could think about is, wow

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.240
I have to sit in this plane
for the next five hours,

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:34.150
It's like this capsule,
I can't get off of it,

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:35.790
and then your brain just starts doing

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:37.070
these very irrational things.

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:40.230
You know, a part of your
brain is saying, hey, brain,

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:42.790
you're being irrational,
chill out, just, you know,

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:45.030
watch a movie and read
a book and take a nap

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:47.250
and you're gonna be in New
York before you know it,

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:50.240
but it's a very unpleasant
feeling for anyone who has gone

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:51.073
through that.

00:19:51.073 --> 00:19:53.890
It's arguably one of the
worst feelings you can have.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:58.890
And, I was talking about this
with a board member of mine

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:02.470
who has to travel frankly, way
more than I have to travel.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:04.140
And he says, "Well, you know,

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:06.660
I actually have the same issue

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.160
and meditation really helped."

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:12.486
And I was like, okay, well, I'll try it.

00:20:12.486 --> 00:20:17.486
(stammers) I didn't want to
resort to having to do any,

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:19.710
you know, medication or anything like that

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:20.543
if I didn't have to.

00:20:20.543 --> 00:20:24.190
So I'm like, let me try meditation.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:26.790
And so, on his recommendation,

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:28.140
I downloaded the Headspace app

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.110
and I started doing that
somewhat religiously.

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:33.600
I did 10 minutes a day
for two, three months

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:35.210
and I kind of started
getting into that pattern.

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:37.170
And then I started to actually
see the benefits of it,

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:40.560
that it really got your mind stilled

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.550
instead of allowing your
mind to kind of race

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:44.010
in a bunch of different directions,

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:46.780
you do, as you get more and more practice,

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:50.280
get to a spot, not all the
time, my mind still wanders,

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:53.700
but more often or more
common than not now,

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:55.450
I'm able to get my mind into a place

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:56.590
where it's kind of still,

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.580
and there's kind of not this thought.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:01.430
And when you do that regularly,

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:03.120
you're less likely to get worked up.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:04.990
You're less likely to get anxious.

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:08.210
Even now, if I'm at work or in other areas

00:21:08.210 --> 00:21:10.370
and a debate is happening

00:21:10.370 --> 00:21:12.060
that normally I would
get very worked up about,

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:13.870
now I'm much more zen about it.

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:17.350
I'm like look, Sal, you
know, do what you need to do,

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:20.020
but don't get too worked up.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.950
These thoughts aren't you.

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:23.720
You aren't defined by whether you win

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:26.570
or lose this debate, et cetera.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:28.740
So anyway, that's how
I got into meditation.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:31.010
I would say after a few
months, I kind of just,

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:33.200
I guess you could say
graduated from the app.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:35.083
And, and now I do,

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:37.910
I try to do at least 30 minutes a day.

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:41.549
And you know, I just sit quietly,

00:21:41.549 --> 00:21:43.223
upright posture and,

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.460
I do kind of a, a body scan, so to speak,

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:49.870
just become aware of my surroundings.

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:53.280
But then I try to just still my mind

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:55.820
and just kind of sit in that moment.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:59.110
And my mind definitely
does still gravitate to,

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:00.510
oh, you got to do this for work,

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:02.090
oh, you forgot to take out the trash,

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:03.957
oh, why did that person say
that to you the other day,

00:22:03.957 --> 00:22:06.210
did they do they mean
what you think they said.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:07.811
So your brain,

00:22:07.811 --> 00:22:09.260
your brain definitely wanders.

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:11.770
But then the other key is laugh it off.

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:14.270
A lot of people take meditation
a little bit too seriously.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:16.170
And then they become stressed out.

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:17.410
Like, you know, you start meditating

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:18.960
and there's like a lawnmower outside.

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:19.950
You're like, "Oh, a lawnmower.

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:21.290
I can't say on the lawnmower!"

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:23.170
And then obviously that
defeats the purpose.

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:25.630
What you really should do is
like, "Oh, that's interesting.

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:26.750
There's a lawnmower.

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:29.890
A lawnmower as part of
this reality, how nice."

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:32.750
So, the more that you smile
externally and internally,

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:35.090
while you meditate in the
morning that you're accepting

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:38.450
of whatever sensations,
whatever thoughts come about,

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.530
you don't try to fight 'em,

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:40.610
it's like, "Oh, look at that thought.

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:42.250
That's not the most positive thought,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:43.220
but it's one of the thoughts.

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:46.262
So here, thought, why don't
you move on a little bit?"

00:22:46.262 --> 00:22:49.030
(laughs) I think that
the healthier it can be.

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:54.030
So, from Facebook, Malini Kishore asks,

00:22:54.407 --> 00:22:55.990
"How can I, as a parent,

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:59.730
instill or encourage curiosity
in my eight and 10 year olds?

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:00.563
Thanks.

00:23:00.563 --> 00:23:02.180
And please expand to older ages too."

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:03.670
So, you know, it's interesting.

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:05.630
'Cause I actually think

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:12.382
all kids have curiosity
pre-installed, so to speak.

00:23:12.382 --> 00:23:14.820
(laughs) So I don't think you
have to instill them with it,

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:15.970
it's already there.

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:18.400
I think what you need to
do is give space for it

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:21.870
and not squeeze it out.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:25.610
And you know, most kids, the
way you give space for it

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:30.370
is when they want to take an
old toilet paper roll or a box

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:31.930
and they want to make
something, as a parent,

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:33.670
and I have to remind this
to myself all the time.

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:37.050
Half the time me or my wife,
we're like, oh, we're busy,

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:38.110
we have to go to this thing,

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:39.560
or you're late for this practice,

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:42.061
or I'm busy, don't bother me,

00:23:42.061 --> 00:23:44.410
why are you making a mess over there.

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:45.970
You know, these are natural parent things

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:48.060
to do every now and then.

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.370
And I have to admit I'm not great at this.

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:52.270
I often fall into what I just described.

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:53.210
I'm like, wait, wait,

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:55.700
this is really cool what
my daughter wants to do.

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:58.580
She wants to cut up that thing

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:01.390
that I was about to throw
away and make this thing.

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:02.750
Maybe I should sit down with her

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:04.300
and work with her a little bit.

00:24:06.139 --> 00:24:08.410
Okay, maybe they'll make
a little bit of a mess

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:09.780
if they try to take those paints

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:11.550
and do this with it on the kitchen counter

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:15.002
and this and that, but maybe
a little bit of that's okay.

00:24:15.002 --> 00:24:18.120
(stammers) I don't practice
what I preach all the time,

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:19.340
just to be very clear.

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:21.430
I'm even thinking about some times

00:24:21.430 --> 00:24:22.530
in my own head over the last week

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:24.640
where I probably shut
down my kids' creativity

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:27.740
by telling them, "Hey, Hey, no
don't make a mess over there.

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:29.110
Wait, wait, we just clean the room"

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:30.050
or, "Wait, wait, why do you want to,

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:31.530
why are you digging in the trash?

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:34.294
Why are you taking that box
out? I just flattened it."

00:24:34.294 --> 00:24:35.570
(laughs) Or whatever else.

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:39.700
So I think giving that
space for them is key.

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:42.340
I think giving, sitting
down with them on the ground

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:44.970
and doing it with them, I
actually think there's a lot

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:47.440
about modeling creativity for them.

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:50.460
A lot of adults kind of
think they're past the age

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:52.330
where they should sit down and draw,

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:53.930
they should sit down and write a poem,

00:24:53.930 --> 00:24:56.060
or sing a song, or compose a song.

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:58.200
So that's another area.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:24:59.670
I think a lot of our,

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:01.470
what in theory our creative pursuits,

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:04.403
like playing a musical instrument,

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:10.380
or visual arts, in our
goal oriented world,

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:12.650
oftentimes become less
creative where you just,

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:13.557
you know, in the musical instrument,

00:25:13.557 --> 00:25:14.920
"Okay, now play a harder piece.

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.686
Okay, now play a harder piece.

00:25:16.686 --> 00:25:18.850
Learn this technique,
learn that technique."

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:20.550
Well, really the musical instruments

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.170
are about creative expression.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.530
And so, you know, I think the more

00:25:24.530 --> 00:25:26.950
that we can emphasize,
say young piano learners,

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:29.360
to also compose their own pieces,

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:31.110
which you don't see in piano class.

00:25:32.458 --> 00:25:33.690
My son has a great piano teacher

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:35.540
and he's doing a great job, but you know,

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:38.270
I definitely encouraged my
son, "Compose your own pieces."

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:39.660
This is about expression.

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:41.700
This is not just about being able

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:43.780
to play a more difficult
piece a little bit faster

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:46.160
and with a little bit more
technical sophistication.

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:47.560
That's useful, too.

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:49.400
That's going to build
your underlying skills,

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:51.490
but there's a whole
creative aspect to this.

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.190
I would say, even in math,

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:55.995
a lot of what you might learn,

00:25:55.995 --> 00:25:58.870
these are the techniques,
these are the tools,

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:01.290
but math is also a creative field.

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:03.010
And so, you know, always have some fun,

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:04.770
enriching problems to do.

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:06.830
Me and my kids, we've been
doing some number theory,

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:08.690
which obviously isn't in
the Common Core standards

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:09.540
or anything,

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:11.580
but they're just these
interesting logic puzzles

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.850
and number theory puzzles
that you have to be,

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:16.010
you have to kind of
have a creative mindset

00:26:16.010 --> 00:26:18.180
to try to think of a solution
or try to think of a proof.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:20.630
So yeah, I would say model it with them,

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:22.630
participate with them, and,

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:24.170
get out of their way a lot of times.

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:26.470
And I would also say
don't over-schedule them.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:31.320
Creativity is oftentimes
a byproduct of boredom.

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:34.940
So a little bit of boredom is good.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:37.070
And over time, any human being
who is sufficiently bored

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:38.320
will figure out something to do,

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:40.320
and that usually is a creative activity.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:46.993
So, this is, let's see,
for other questions.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:50.187
From Facebook, Julie Ramos Jones.

00:26:50.187 --> 00:26:51.300
"I have a junior in high school

00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:53.590
who will be starting
distance learning in August.

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:54.770
What can I do to help her succeed,

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:56.350
accomplishing her graduation requirements

00:26:56.350 --> 00:26:58.390
through distance learning?"

00:26:58.390 --> 00:27:00.360
So, Julie, I don't know all of your,

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:03.343
all of your daughter's circumstances.

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:05.850
So I think on the learning side,

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.640
a lot of the advice that I gave
to the middle school student

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:09.860
a couple of minutes ago,

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:11.690
I think would apply to your daughter.

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:14.980
You know, math, we've got her
back, whatever level she's at,

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:16.390
whether she's at trigonometry,

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:19.970
pre-calculus, calculus,
stats, work on Khan Academy.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:22.890
I would say two 30 minute sessions a day

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:24.180
would be incredible.

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:26.420
Hopefully she has support from her school

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:29.173
or her peers or from you if
she gets stuck on anything.

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:31.690
Actually, there's this other effort

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:32.870
that I've talked about here before

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:35.110
called schoolhouse.world,

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:37.450
which is a, not a Khan Academy effort,

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:38.310
it's a separate effort,

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:40.780
maybe one day it becomes
a Khan Academy effort,

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:44.050
but it's to pair students
who need help with a subject,

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:45.550
especially in math,
we're starting in math,

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:46.970
but eventually might go to other subjects

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.840
and volunteer tutors running
group tutoring sessions.

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:51.640
So that's another layer of support

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.320
that we might be able to also provide,

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:56.380
or that the side project
might be able to provide,

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:57.770
schoolhouse.world.

00:27:57.770 --> 00:27:59.550
So between Khan Academy,

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:01.300
she should be able to get
enough practice in math

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:02.650
and then get these other supports.

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:05.710
And then in science, we
also have got her back.

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:09.240
We have high school and AP
level physics, chemistry,

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.500
biology, econ, also in the humanities,

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:15.240
American history and
government and politics.

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:17.070
I'd say all of that content is perfect

00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:19.620
for the age that your daughter's at.

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:21.610
If the question is
around credit, you know,

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:24.350
this is gonna be really
interesting this year,

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:27.010
where colleges are going to
have a lot less information.

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:29.090
A lot of schools have gone to pass/fail,

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:31.370
or they're just passing
everyone because of,

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:35.300
you know, inequity to access
of devices and things.

00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:37.470
And so I think the successful students

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:39.080
are going to have to come
up with alternate ways

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:42.350
of showing colleges that they've made it

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:44.169
or that they've not made it, that they've,

00:28:44.169 --> 00:28:46.600
they've met their requirements

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:48.350
and that they know the material.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:49.770
Meeting the requirements, most,

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:51.730
if you think about like the
state university systems,

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:54.980
most state university systems
have an alternative way

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:57.260
to prove your competencies.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:59.690
Most private schools,
at the end of the day,

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:04.020
tend to be less rigid about,
most private colleges,

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:06.220
tend to be less rigid
about their requirements.

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:08.500
More that they want to see
the, are you college ready?

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:12.993
Are you a caring person who
wants to help the world?

00:29:13.968 --> 00:29:15.910
What are your capabilities
in different subject areas?

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:17.110
And there's a lot of
ways you can show that.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:18.440
You can do that with SAT.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:19.920
Maybe you can even show your mastery.

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:23.460
We had Jim Nondorf from
the University of Chicago

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:25.510
on this live stream a
couple of months ago.

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:28.000
And you know, Jim and I have
been talking about, well,

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:30.670
he would love to see kids submit
their Khan Academy mastery

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:31.900
because not only would that,

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:34.250
let's say your daughter able
is able to get 90% mastery

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:36.060
on calculus on Khan Academy,

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:38.480
if she wanted to apply
to University Chicago,

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:40.570
and she submitted that,
from Jim's point of view,

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:42.430
not only that be a pretty good indication

00:29:42.430 --> 00:29:43.970
that she knows her calculus,

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:45.760
but it also is an indication

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:48.230
that she's a self-learner,
she has a lot of agency.

00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:49.990
You know, even in a tough time like COVID

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:52.260
when a lot of things are hit and miss,

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.580
she's able to drive her
own learning forward,

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:55.850
which I think would look good.

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:59.660
I also think people
don't use video enough.

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:01.960
A video is a great way
to advocate for yourself.

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:03.650
One thing that I do, and
the few times, you know,

00:30:03.650 --> 00:30:06.270
every now and then whether it's a student

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.380
that I've worked with or
whatever wants a recommendation,

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:10.090
I actually make
recommendation videos for them

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:13.063
because it becomes, you can't fake that.

00:30:13.063 --> 00:30:14.310
(laughs) It's like, you know,

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:16.940
it's not like someone else
wrote it and just signed it.

00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:18.870
And I would say you can
advocate yourself in that

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:20.140
for yourself in that same way.

00:30:20.140 --> 00:30:21.860
If you can create video artifacts,

00:30:21.860 --> 00:30:24.030
that show things that you've created,

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:26.490
that show you explaining concepts,

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:30.550
then that only adds to your,
to your portfolio to show

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:32.310
that not only do you know the material,

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:33.990
not only have you done
cool, creative things,

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.500
but you also know how to
communicate your work.

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:37.990
And I think that is also another way

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:40.610
to self-advocate frankly,
at any age, at any age.

00:30:40.610 --> 00:30:42.970
So anyway, those are my, my ideas.

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:45.630
I am trying to talk to state leaders,

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:49.230
college folks for what
if Khan Academy itself,

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:51.220
mastery on Khan Academy could count

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:53.500
towards the state
requirements for graduation

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:56.236
or towards the state
university requirements.

00:30:56.236 --> 00:30:58.580
Maybe there's a way that
a teacher can validate

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:00.620
that it's the student's
work, proctor them some way.

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:02.760
Maybe you could take a course
challenge on Khan Academy

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:06.220
and talk out loud on audio
while you're doing it

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:07.440
and submit the audio too.

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:09.140
So people like, "Look,
I clearly did this work.

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:10.780
I'm thinking out loud
doing every problem."

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:12.558
That's hard to fake.

00:31:12.558 --> 00:31:13.391
So stuff like that,

00:31:13.391 --> 00:31:14.420
but it's going to be an
interesting challenge,

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:16.070
especially if COVID lasts,

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:17.390
and it looks like it very well could last

00:31:17.390 --> 00:31:20.010
for another year or even longer.

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:21.580
So, we're all out of time.

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:23.133
This is these, these always go,

00:31:24.290 --> 00:31:28.100
these always go super fast.

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:30.400
So I'll answer one last
question from Facebook.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:32.650
Selma Malek says, "My eight year old

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:34.020
is interested in coding,

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:36.060
but I don't want to encourage
too much screen time.

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:36.900
He likes Scratch.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:37.733
What else should he do?

00:31:37.733 --> 00:31:39.100
How old are your kids?"

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:41.870
Well, my kids are 11, nine and 5 1/2,

00:31:41.870 --> 00:31:44.030
and they are also into coding.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:45.200
They've used scratch.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:46.070
They've used Tinker.

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:47.920
They've used the Khan Academy coding.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:52.890
Screen time is an interesting debate.

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:56.520
I think if a child is doing
really creative things

00:31:56.520 --> 00:32:00.330
like writing on a screen,
like writing a paper

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:03.960
or a blog post, or if
they're editing video,

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:06.050
or if they are coding,

00:32:06.050 --> 00:32:07.700
that is a good use of screen time.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:10.230
And so for child that is eager to code,

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:12.150
I would say, yes,
definitely let them code.

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:13.930
But the more important thing is make sure

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:15.840
that they're also getting
other healthy things

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:17.310
in their life, making sure

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.180
that they're spending
a good amount of time

00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:21.700
getting their vitamin D out
in the sun, running around,

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:23.860
getting breathless as much as possible

00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:26.020
in this COVID-19 socially distanced world;

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:28.220
they're able to interact
with other people,

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:30.010
have social connections,

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:32.350
that they have just
free time to themselves;

00:32:32.350 --> 00:32:33.540
that there's time with the family,

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:35.230
that they're eating right, sleeping right.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:38.000
If they're doing all of
that stuff, coding is great.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:40.410
And it's a great outlet creativity.

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:42.787
You know, I remember I didn't,

00:32:42.787 --> 00:32:46.003
(stammers) I didn't
start coding until later.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.320
But it was an incredible
outlet for creativity for me.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:53.670
And sometimes when I was
really into creating a new game

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:57.990
or a new app, I would
spend 12 hours straight.

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:01.140
And I think almost anyone
who's really immersed

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:03.360
in a creative field will tell you

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.460
they spend 12 hours
straight at least sometimes

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:07.400
when they're really in the zone.

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:08.670
And there's nothing wrong with that.

00:33:08.670 --> 00:33:10.070
In fact, I think that's good,

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:11.750
once again, as long as
they're eating right,

00:33:11.750 --> 00:33:13.730
they're getting their exercise,
they're running outside,

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:16.390
they're not only on a screen all day,

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:19.690
then I think, you know,
the bad screen time

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:22.190
is where you're kind of just vegged out,

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:24.660
watching something and you're just kinda,

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:25.530
you know, whatever.

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:26.970
That's probably the stuff that,

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:29.653
watching people unpackage
toys or something,

00:33:29.653 --> 00:33:31.450
(laughs) that can be or worse,

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:33.790
that could be a little less productive.

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:35.620
But anyway, thanks everyone.

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:36.480
These are really good questions.

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:38.993
I'll try to get to them on the next AMA.

00:33:38.993 --> 00:33:40.810
Just a reminder to everyone

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:43.530
who was expecting to
see Anthony Fauci today,

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:47.390
they had to reschedule to Friday.

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:49.390
So, still very excited
about that conversation.

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:51.960
We're going to be talking
about obviously COVID

00:33:51.960 --> 00:33:54.280
and school closures related to COVID.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:56.440
So very, very excited
about that conversation.

00:33:56.440 --> 00:33:57.880
So thanks everyone for joining.

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:00.660
I really enjoy these "Ask Me Anythings"

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:02.910
and look forward to
seeing you all on Friday.

